    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d960">

                <pb facs="1223" n="1209" sameAs="frus:frus1945v09/pg1209"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.248/10–2945</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Telephone Conversation, by the Chief of
                        the Division of Caribbean and Central American Affairs</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Cochran</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-10-29">October 29, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Ambassador Warren telephoned this morning to state that he was somewhat concerned
                    at a situation which had arisen, as he was informed that the Nicaraguan
                    Government had sent two men to Panama to buy two primary training planes and two
                    basic trainers for cash. He said that this was definitely not good and that it
                    would give an erroneous and extremely unfortunate impression for these four
                    planes to arrive in Nicaragua within the next few days. He said he feared there
                    was nothing which could be done at this late date. I said that I would look into
                    the matter immediately.<anchor n="33" xml:id="d960-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W[<hi rendition="#small-caps">illiam</hi>] P. C[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ochran</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d960-f1" n="33" xml:id="d960-f1-content">
                <p>Ambassador Warren reported to the Department in airgram A–416, November 5, 1945,
                    that as a result of the Department’s efforts the sale was not consummated and
                    that the Nicaraguans had returned to Managua without the desired planes
                    (817.796/11–545).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
